A1. You never see these on a snake. ~ HIPS
A4. A face (ringside lingo). ~ MUG
A6. When Cupid does this at a miss he usually Mrs. her. ~ AIMS
A9. To create. ~ MAKE
A11. The Pied Piper's audience (pl.). ~ RATS
A13. Preposition. ~ TO
A15. The standard by which the volume or extent of anything is compared. ~ MEASURE
A17. In the year of our Lord (Latin abr.). ~ AD
A18. A Greek letter (not a billet-doux). ~ PI
A20. It is claimed we must do this for our sins. ~ ATONE
A21. Correlative of either. ~ OR
A22. What a ball player wants to be at first. ~ SAFE
A24. Pay day (abr.). ~ SAT
A25. Something no barber can do without using gas. ~ CLIP
A27. He (French). ~ IL
A28. Prefix denoting repetition. ~ RE
A30. Proceed. ~ GO
A31. The beginning and end of the vocal exercise. ~ DO
A32. Correct answer to "letsavanother." ~ SURE
A33. A larger bundle. ~ BALE
A34. Civil engineer (abr.). ~ CE
A36. The old block itself. ~ PA
A38. Something that runs between sidewalks (abr.), ask any pedestrian. ~ ST
A39. You don't have to go to Holland to get — Dutch. ~ IN
A40. A direction no young Californian is advised to go. ~ EAST
A42. A period of time. ~ ERA
A44. Something Lady Macbeth profanely requested to get out. ~ SPOT
A46. A New England State (abr.). ~ RI
A47. What is competition the life of? ~ TRADE
A49. Neuter pronoun. ~ IT
A50. Chemical symbol for a member of the halogen group that caused a lot of trouble in the World War. ~ CL
A51. What you walk on when you cross the ocean (two words). ~ SEALEGS
A53. Nearby or on. ~ AT
A55. Things that are cooling but not when applied to baseball. ~ FANS
A56. No, Dora, you can't swim in this kind of a room. ~ POOL
A58. An arciferous, insectiverous bufonid amphibian that is not troubled by pyorrhea. ~ TOAD
A59. The kind that drive a man to drink are always leading him to a place to do this. ~ EAT
A60. Reformer's slogan. ~ STOP

D1. A seventy-dollar "creation" made from about seventy cents' worth of material. ~ HAT
D2. After lunch. ~ PM
D3. Our benevolent uncle. ~ SAM
D4. Something we like about a stew (pl.). ~ MEATS
D5. The morning greeting of one porker to another. ~ GRUNT
D6. Consumed. ~ ATE
D7. Present indicative of the verb be. ~ IS
D8. Something all of us will eventually be under. ~ SOD
D10. A New Zealand parrot. ~ KEA
D12. Present tense plural of the verb be. ~ ARE
D14. A precious stone. ~ OPAL
D16. Something to be used around the neck. ~ SOAP
D17. What a dry country is. ~ ARID
D19. Foundation of air castles. ~ IF
D21. Old Latin (abr.). ~ OL
D22. From that time. ~ SINCE
D23. Something that lively volcanoes do. ~ ERUPT
D25. What do horses have that no other animals do (pl.)? ~ COLTS
D26. Something often very hard to see. ~ POINT
D29. The same as 42 Horizontal. ~ ERA
D30. Empty talk. ~ GAS
D35. English nobleman. ~ EARL
D37. Verbal. ~ ORAL
D39. A Greek letter used to denote smallness. ~ IOTA
D41. A note of the scale. ~ SI
D42. An optimist doesn't expect to do this when he starts a crossword puzzle with a fountain pen. ~ ERASE
D43. Skillful. ~ ADEPT
D45. A mix-up in a printing plant. ~ PI
D47. Something that comes before eleven in the morning. ~ TEN
D48. Self. ~ EGO
D50. Something that goes out at night. ~ CAT
D51. Mournful. ~ SAD
D52. The cry of a sinking schooner. ~ SOS
D54. It goes around a lot. ~ TOP
D55. A musical note. ~ FA
D57. Lieutenant (abr.). ~ LT


